    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe E.164 rule lives in one place \u2014 `OwnerTelephones.toE164` \u2014 which both `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` (stores the value) and `RejectDuplicateTelephone` (compares against it) call, so they can't drift. I added the per-country national-length check there:\n\n- Introduced a `NATIONAL_DIGITS` map (ordered longest-prefix first): `\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`.\n- After the existing 8\u201315 digit bound, it finds the matching country code by prefix and requires the national number (digits minus the country code) to be exactly that length; a recognised country code with the wrong length now returns `null`, which the pipeline turns into an `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 **400**.\n\nFor the failing test case `+61 123` \u2192 cleaned `+61123` \u2192 `61123` (5 digits), which is caught and rejected. A correct `+61` number carries 11 total digits (9 national), and `+1` carries 11 total (10 national) \u2014 both still accepted. The default-country path (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national) remains valid.\n\nI also updated the javadoc on `OwnerTelephones`/`NormalizeOwnerTelephone` and the `InvalidTelephoneException` message/doc to state the per-country rule.\n\nTest result:\n```\nTests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0\nBUILD SUCCESS\n```\n\n(No git repo here, so nothing to commit.)",
